---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
---

<Page
  title="Rights and licences"
  description="Who owns what: authors keep copyright, the site takes a non-exclusive licence to publish, code is permissive, and every data source states its terms."
  current="/rights/"
>
  <h1>Rights and licences</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    This site does not own contributed work and does not ask to. It needs permission to
    publish and archive, and nothing more.
  </p>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Ownership<span>element by element</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Who holds what</h2>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Held by</th><th>Licence</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>The finding, the interpretation, the written discussion</td>
              <td><b>the researcher</b></td><td>CC BY 4.0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pipeline and dashboard code</td>
              <td><b>the studio</b></td><td>MIT</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Upstream data</td>
              <td>the original provider</td><td>stated per source, on every case</td></tr>
            <tr><td>This website</td>
              <td>openstudy.id</td><td>site code and design only</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          What the site takes is a <b>non-exclusive, perpetual licence to publish and
          archive</b>. You can republish the same work anywhere, put it on your own site,
          submit it to a journal, or fold it into a thesis — without asking.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="dim">
        Journals routinely require an exclusive copyright transfer. Asking for nothing
        costs this site nothing, and it is one of the few concrete advantages it can offer
        before it has any audience to trade on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Data<span>fetched, not redistributed</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Nothing here redistributes somebody else's dataset</h2>
      <p>
        Pipelines fetch from the origin every time, and each source's licence is named on
        the case that uses it. What gets published is the derived result — counts, model
        outputs, figures — not a copy of the input.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is partly principle and partly self-protection: a site that redistributes data
        takes on every upstream licence question, and a small operation cannot carry that.
        It also means a source withdrawing its data breaks the pipeline honestly rather
        than leaving a stale copy circulating.
      </p>
      <div class="aside">
        Where a source's terms are unclear, it is not used. Where they permit use with
        attribution, the attribution is on the page — not in a footnote on this one.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Copyright<span>what actually binds</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Copyright constrains you, not the venue</h2>
      <p>
        If you signed an exclusive transfer to a publisher, that binds you wherever you
        publish next — a figure you cannot reuse here is one you cannot reuse on your own
        site either. It is not a reason to publish elsewhere; it travels with you.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice it rarely bites here, because instruments are built from <b>the data
        and the code</b>, never from a publisher's typeset PDF. Figures are re-rendered
        from source. Nothing reproduces the article. Where a paper is open access under
        CC BY, reuse is explicit anyway.
      </p>
      <h3>What you confirm</h3>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          "I hold the rights to the data and code published here, and this artifact is
          compatible with my publisher's or target journal's policy."
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        That is the whole declaration. This site does not interpret publisher contracts —
        it cannot, and guessing wrong would be your problem rather than its. The judgement
        stays with the only person who has read the agreement.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Personal data<span>none</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>No personal data, ever</h2>
      <p>
        Aggregate only. No names, identity numbers, addresses, contact details or anything
        else that identifies a person — in any published file, not just in the visible page.
        It is checked mechanically on every case before publication.
      </p>
      <p class="dim">
        This is a scope limit rather than a capability claim. Handling personal data
        properly means obligations a volunteer-run site should not be taking on, so the
        answer is to not hold any.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
